Wait pin at head rear belongs to head, but body1 socket cavity is separate. The head arm ends at pin; pin flanges and shaft. Good.
Head body 2D hull with big circle at e.g. (10,0)r11, rear (13,0)r8, snout (1.5,0)r2. This hull extends x -1 to21; clip at x>=0 and <=20? Need overall head body not extend beyond x20 (to leave gap to ring at28). Big circle center10 r11 => x -1..21, y±11; rear circle center12 r8 =>4..20; snout center1.5 r2. The hull left boundary -1. If intersection x>=0, front flat at x=0 width? Could look chopped. We can shift circles so left extent0: big circle center11 r11 -> x0..22, rear center12 r8->4..20 (big still22). Need body end20, so big r10 center10 ->0..20, max width20. Head width20 not 24. Rear circle center12,r8 ->4..20. Hull max width20 at x10. Head still within body width18 slightly wider. Fine. Snout circle at (2,0) r2 -> x0..4. Hull of big (10,0)r10, rear (12,0)r8, snout(2,0)r2. Big circle at x10 extends to x0,y0 but max width at x10±10. This is an oval head length20 width20. Good. The head max width20, body width18, subtle. Could make big r11 center9 => x -2..20, clip x>=0, head width22 and snout chopped at x0 with width? At x0, circle center9 r11 gives y=±sqrt(121-81)=±6.32. That's a blunt front 12.6mm wide, not snout. Add snout circle at0,r2 still hull within big. We want pointed snout; hull of big circle at10 r10 (leftmost x=0 only at y0) and snout at2 r2 yields leftmost x=0 from big y0 and snout x0; the hull boundary near x0 is maybe cusp point, good pointed. So big r10 center10. Head pointed at x0,y0, widens to20 at x10, rear tapers to20 at x20? Rear circle center12 r8 extends to20; hull of big and rear gives width maybe16 at x18. Overall head shape good. Arm attaches at x18-28. At x20, body only point y0. Head overall body from0..20, arm to28.

Eyes at (8,±7)? Head width at x8 roughly? big circle width y±sqrt(100-4)=9.8, eye r2 at y7.5 fits. On top z10, small cylinders h0.8, radius1.8. Maybe use black not possible. Add eye discs and maybe vertical pupil? Not necessary.

Head body maybe add lower jaw? We can add a small chin? no.

Body part module:
```
module body_part(i) {
  front = 0;
  // B socket ring at front, body local x R_SEP..L-R_SEP
  difference() {
    union() {
      // ring outer disk
      translate([0,0,0]) cylinder(h=T, r=R_RING);
      // neck
      linear_extrude(T) neck_2d();
      // main body 2D
      linear_extrude(T) body_2d(i);
      // rear pin (if i<5)
      if (i<5) translate([L,0,0]) pin_solid();
      // rear arm
      if (i<5) linear_extrude(T) arm_2d(...);
    }
    // socket cavity (subtract full height)
    socket_cavity();
    // mouth cut
    mouth_cut();
  }
}
```
Need subtract socket cavity only at front. `socket_cavity()` itself at origin. This will also cut body material if overlapping; intended.
Order: subtract cavity and mouth from union. But if body_2d extends left of x8? For tail maybe no. Body rect x8..18. Neck x6.5..10; mouth cut on left won't hit neck. Good.
For body5 tail body_2d from x8 to26. union ring/neck. subtract cavity/mouth. No rear pin/arm.
For body i=1..4, body_2d x8..18; arm to L; pin at L. The arm/pin should not be cut by front cavity/mouth (far). Fine.

But B ring at front plus body/neck: the body may be too far right; ring connected by neck. When `difference` subtracts socket cavity, it also subtracts a cylindrical volume r5.5 bottom/top etc from neck if neck overlaps x6.5,y0 (r6.5 >5.5, not cut) and body x8. Good.

Mouth wedge cut extends through z; it may cut neck? wedge x<0 only, no.

`body_2d(i)`: if i<5 rounded rect x R_SEP..L-R_SEP (8..18), y±BODY_W/2=9. For body5 tail polygon. Need union with `neck` x=6.5..10 y±3.
Rounded body rect via hull of 4 circles radius2 at (10,±7),(16,±7) gives x8..18 y±9. For i<5.
Tail polygon maybe start x8,-9 to16,-9,22,-5,26,0,22,5,16,9,8,9. Could round using offset but okay. Maybe body5 tail shape should start x8 to16 full width then taper. Use polygon with vertex at (26,0). Since union with neck. At x8,y±9 abrupt vertical edge; body front at x8, ring gap .5. That's fine. For aesthetics, body5 could have rounded corners; not critical.

Tail end at x=L=26, pointed. Overall x = front132+26=158.

Pin module profile:
```
module pin_solid() {
  rotate_extrude(convexity=10, $fn=96)
    polygon(points=[
      [0,0],[R_FLANGE,0],[R_FLANGE,Z_BFLANGE],[R_PIN,Z_BFLANGE],
      [R_PIN,Z_TCONE],[R_FLANGE,T],[0,T]
    ]);
}
```
Need polygon order. Starting at (0,0) -> (5,0) -> (5,1.5) -> (3.5,1.5) -> (3.5,7.7) -> (5,10) -> (0,10) -> back to (0,0). This is valid CCW? In x,z plane, starting bottom center to bottom right, up, left, up, right, top center, down. This is CCW around solid? Shoelace positive? likely. OpenSCAD rotate_extrude requires polygon not self-intersecting; orientation doesn't matter for solid maybe. `rotate_extrude` takes polygon in XY (x radial, y height); `polygon` points (x, z). Good.
Top cone profile from (R_PIN,7.7) to (R_FLANGE,10). Bottom flange top horizontal from5 to3.5 at z1.5. This creates a flat shoulder but it's inside and faces upward; no issue.
Pin base at z0.

Socket cavity profile:
points [(0,0),(R_CB,0),(R_CB,2),(R_HOLE,3.5),(R_HOLE,6.5),(R_CB,8),(R_CB,10),(0,10)].
This is the void. Rotate extrude.
Potential issue subtract cavity with exact bottom z0: OpenSCAD difference may leave zero-thickness faces at bottom? The cavity boundary at z=0 matches part bottom, subtract open at bottom. Usually okay. For manifold, subtraction of a volume that starts exactly at bottom still yields open hole through bottom. Fine.
Mouth cut z from -1 to T+1 to ensure full. It cuts through entire B ring and maybe body if in wedge. Use linear_extrude(height=T+2, center? translate z=-1). 
Need `mouth polygon` with center. Since $fn=72, angle steps maybe 2.5°.
```
module mouth_cut() {
  translate([0,0,-1])
  linear_extrude(height=T+2, convexity=10)
  polygon(points=concat([[0,0]], [for (a=[105:3:255]) [100*cos(a),100*sin(a)]]));
}
```
Should the wedge be only outer ring and not center? Including center creates opening all the way. Good. But note it subtracts a wedge to radius100; if body x>0 no. At angles 105/255, x=100*cos105=-25.9, y±96.6; cuts far left. Fine. It will cut any part at far left, but B body is right.
Could reduce mouth radius to20; ring radius7.5 and arm/clearance; 20 enough. Use 50.

Now collision. We need ensure arms/pins don't intersect socket when rotated. The pin is rotationally symmetric, B socket rotationally symmetric except mouth/body. Rotating A around Z while pin axis same: pin and cavity are coaxial, so no interference from socket hole (clearance .5). The only possible interference is A body/arm near B ring/body; B ring is C. At ±25, arm in mouth. At the mouth edges, arm width5. At inner radius4 (hole), but arm exists only outside pin? The arm rectangle width5 from x=-8..0. At x near0, its corners y=±2.5, r2.5 (inside hole r4) no B material. At r4, arm extends to x=-sqrt(16-6.25)=-3.12; angle relative -x: atan(2.5/3.12)=38.7. With rotation25, edge angle63.7 < mouth75. Good. At r5.5, arm half-angle asin2.5/5.5=27; +25=52<75. So arm clears mouth with18° margin near inner. The B C ring mouth half75; good. What about arm root at x=-8,y±2.5 r8.38>R_RING7.5, so no ring. B body right at x>=8, no. A body end x=-8 (local body end relative front? At rear A body ends at joint-8), its front? A body width18 and right edge x=-8, distance8>7.5. At rotation ±25, B ring remains r<=7.5, so clearance0.5 to A body edge. Good. B body/neck at x>=6.5; A body left. Could B body rotate -25 and reach A body? B body front edge at local x=8 (right of joint), y up to9. Point (8,-9) rotated -25 (clockwise) -> x'=8*0.906 + (-9)*(-? Let's define rotation angle +25 CCW: x'=x cos - y sin, y'=x sin+y cos. For θ=-25 sin=-.423: x'=8*.906 - (-9)*(-.423)=7.25-3.81=3.44; y'=8*(-.423)+(-9)*.906=-3.38-8.15=-11.53. Still x positive. Point (8,9) at θ=-25: x'=7.25+3.81=11.06. So B body swings x>=3.4, no A body at x<=-8. Safe. B neck at x6.5..10,y±3: at θ=-25, x' for (6.5,3)=5.89- (-?) x'=6.5*.906 -3*(-.423)=5.89+1.27=7.16; for (6.5,-3)=5.89-1.27=4.62. Still positive. No A body. So body-body collision unlikely.

But A arm length from x=-8 to0. When A rotates ±25, its root at (-8,±2.5) could approach B ring/neck? B ring fixed at neutral for relative test. Root radius8.38>7.5, mouth? At angle ±? For x=-8,y=2.5 angle162.6, mouth 105-255 yes. Rotating ±25 relative: angle137.6/187.6, both in mouth. At root radius8.38>7.5, outside ring anyway. The arm crosses B annulus at intermediate radius; mouth enough. Good.

Joint motion ±25 with 0.5 clearance. We can choose designed range ±30 to be safe, document expected ±30 with requirement ≥25. But mouth margin at inner r4: 63.7 at 25, at 30=>68.7 <75. So could allow30. However arm root? at r? Fine. We can set expected ±30 maybe, but requirement expected movement range should state at least25. joints.json can say min -30 max +30, maybe verify at ±30. But if geometry allows30 with margin6°, good. Need also prevent B ring mouth edge collision with A arm at +30: inner edge69<75. Clearance 6° corresponds at r4 to0.42mm, close to0.4. Better expected ±25 or ±27 with margin. Requirement "至少25°" so declare ±25, verify at ±25 plus maybe ±27. Use ±25 expected; actual max maybe30.

Need also check axial retention geometry: maybe analyze cross-section, state capture. We'll compute axial separation attempt by simulating translate? Not necessary but document.

Need model.stl "neutral straight pose complete model". We can export all parts union. Since parts separated, union results multiple shells. Good.

Need `parts/parts_1.stl` etc. Requirement says "parts/*.stl ... 用序号命名，例如 parts_1.stl". We'll use parts_1..parts_6.

Need `joints.json` declarations: head, 5 bodies, connections, axes, positions, expected motion. Need decide schema. Mention part IDs, file paths, joint origin [x,y,z], axis [0,0,1], parent/child, range degrees ±25, type revolute, clearances. Include coordinate system mm, neutral pose, bbox. Could include neutral transforms identity. Independent motion verification expects perhaps joints list with part IDs. Need make clear.

Need extra files: `README.md` or `print_orientation_and_checks.md` with print direction and actual geometric/slicing checks. Could also include validation script or reports: `validation_report.json`, `slicer/` maybe. Requirement says other files go output. We can generate `CHECK_REPORT.md`, maybe `slicer_job.log`, gcode. Need perform actual slice and report. We'll create `print_orientation.md` with direction: all parts on bed, Z up, no supports, 0.2 layer, 0.4 nozzle, PLA, 3 perimeters maybe. The provided config no support likely. We should inspect config for supports/brim. Slice model.stl no supports, save gcode and stats. Use prusa-slicer CLI. Check result for layer count, time, filament, no support. Could parse gcode comments. Need maybe use config and `--dont-arrange`? Single model. Need ensure print-in-place parts not fused. Slice info and possibly inspect gcode for support markers. Check `; support_material = 0` maybe. Use `prusa-slicer --export-gcode --load nosupport ...`.

Need run geometric checks with trimesh:
1. Each part watertight, volume >0, bbox.
2. Combined bbox <=160,50,40.
3. Parts disjoint in neutral (min distance >= something; actual gaps .5).
4. At each joint, rotate child and parent? To validate joint movement, keep ancestor fixed and rotate child subtree? For testing joint i, rotate all parts distal to joint around joint by ±25, while proximal fixed. Check no collision between any moved part and unmoved? Since other distal joints at neutral, entire distal chain moves as rigid around joint i; all distal parts move together, so only interface between part i-1 (or head for joint1) and part i (body i) matters, but other distal parts could collide with proximal due to large swing? At 25, unlikely. We can test all moved vs unmoved pairs.

Part numbering: parts_1=head, parts_2=body1,...parts_6=body5. Joint1 connects head(1) to body1(2), joint2 body1(2)-body2(3), ..., joint5 body4(5)-body5(6). For joint j, proximal parts 0..j-1 (indices), distal j..5. Rotate distal by angle around joint center.
Need collision check between meshes. Trimesh boolean intersection can be expensive but meshes moderate. Use `trimesh.boolean.intersection([meshA, meshB], engine='manifold')` or trimesh.collision? `manifold3d` installed. Use `trimesh.boolean.intersection`. If volume > tiny => collision. But at exactly 0 clearance? We have .5. Also compute minimum distance with trimesh.proximity? `trimesh.proximity.closest_point` one direction not symmetric; use scipy cKDTree sample or `trimesh.proximity.signed_distance`. Simpler for collision, boolean. For min distance, use `trimesh.proximity.closest_point` combinations or `rtree`/`fcl`? We can sample surfaces? Need not exact. Can use `trimesh.proximity.closest_point(meshA, meshB.vertices)` to get distances from B vertices to A surface; and vice versa. But closest approach may be edge-edge not vertex, though for our planar features likely. Better use `scipy.spatial.cKDTree` on dense surface sample points and query both ways; min sampled distance approximate. Could sample 20k points each via trimesh.sample.sample_surface. That gives reliable min gap ~0.5. But maybe not necessary.

Need check radial gaps directly from geometry dimensions: .5. Maybe measure distance between pin and socket surfaces? Could construct cross-section. But can report design values and mesh min distances at joint.

Need maybe verify at ±25 no collision and min clearance positive >0.3. Use boolean and sampled distance. We can produce validation JSON with collision volumes and min distances.

Need inspect actual model after generating to ensure visually good. Use OpenSCAD export PNG? no display but can render via xvfb-run openscad to PNG; could use matplotlib 3D or trimesh screenshot not available. We can use OpenSCAD `--render` to PNG with camera, under xvfb. Or use `xvfb-run openscad -o preview.png --imgsize=... --camera=... --viewall`. OpenSCAD can output PNG headless maybe. We can inspect with read_image. Good for visual.

